course: dictionary
lesson: 12322
title: Astronaut or cosmonaut?
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A cosmonaut is an astronaut from Russia or, in the past, from the Soviet Union. Both words mean a person trained to travel into space. «The cosmonaut floated inside the space station.» The word tells you that the space traveller is from Russia or the Soviet Union.
explain: Look for context clues. In «The Russian cosmonaut trained for years before her flight», the word Russian is the clue that shows where the cosmonaut comes from. Other clues can be a date, a country name, or a space mission.
explain: The Soviet Union was a large country that existed in the past. Russia exists today. So a cosmonaut can be from either time, but the word always points to Russia or the Soviet Union.
fact: A cosmonaut is an astronaut from Russia or, in the past, from the Soviet Union.
fact: Yuri Gagarin, a Soviet cosmonaut, was the first person in space, in 1961.
fact: Valentina Tereshkova was the first woman in space, in 1963.
fact: Different countries use different words: astronaut is used in the USA, Europe and most countries, and cosmonaut is used for Russia.
fact: Some people use the word taikonaut for a space traveller from China.
exercise: «The cosmonaut floated inside the space station.» What is a cosmonaut? | A person who sells food to astronauts | A rocket that sits on the launch pad | *A person who travels into space, from Russia or the Soviet Union | A scientist who studies stars only from Earth
exercise: Yuri Gagarin was a Soviet cosmonaut. Which country does the word Soviet point to? | The United States | China | Germany | *The Soviet Union
exercise: Dana says a space traveller from the USA should be called a cosmonaut. What is wrong with this? | Cosmonaut means a rocket | Nothing, the words are the same in every country | Cosmonaut means a person from the USA | *Cosmonaut is mostly used for Russia, and the USA usually says astronaut
test: Which cosmonaut was the first woman in space? | *Valentina Tereshkova | Amelia Earhart | Yuri Gagarin
test: «The Russian cosmonaut trained for years before her flight.» What does the word Russian tell you? | *The cosmonaut comes from Russia | The flight was very short | The cosmonaut is a cook on the station | The cosmonaut is from the USA
exam: A child says every astronaut is a cosmonaut. Is that right? | No, cosmonauts never go into space | Yes, but only in the USA | Yes, the two words mean exactly the same for everyone | *No, a cosmonaut is an astronaut from Russia or the Soviet Union
